parser_ext/README

Explicit Extension Blocks for Arbitrary Syntax
==============================================

This is a prototype for arbitrary syntax support via explicit extension blocks
with user-provided parsers.

Background
----------

Postgres already supports rich variety of user extensions, ranging from the
family of procedural languages (PL/...) and to the system of code hooks.
Support for syntax extension blocks would complement the existing
infrastructure, allowing, among other things, experimentation with
domain-specific languages and concise declarative annotations without changes
to the core syntax. One can interpret it as integration of the user-provided
parser into a subgrammar of the core parser.

For instance, this would enable custom annotations for columns in CREATE
statements such as LOW CARDINALITY N and fillfactor(10).

Interface and Behavior
----------------------

The syntax for an extension block is the following:

  SYNTAX EXTENSION <'optional parser name'> ('source text')

Extensions can register new parser by calling
RegisterSyntaxExtensionParser(name, parser). If a registration with the same
name exists, the existing parser will be replaced with the provided one.
Parser registry is local to the current session.

Conceptually extension blocks can be divided into 2 categories:

1. Separate statement. This is a top-level statement, like SELECT or CREATE
TABLE, and the extension block is expected to return a Node pointer with one
of the ...Stmt nodes. Extension-provided parsers are invoked during the
grammatical analysis. By convention parser function responsible for such
statement is prefixed with parse_.

2. Part of non-terminal at curated entry point. Extension-provided handlers
are invoked during the post-processing of the raw parse tree. Handlers return
nothing: they operate on the Node corresponding to the current non-terminal,
potentially transforming it. Handlers are also provided with supplementary
context inferred during the post-processing. Reported errors have proper error
position reporting. By convention such handler functions are prefixed with
process_.

As of now the only entry point is column definition, and its handlers are
provided with ColumnDef structure and with relation which this column is
part of.

If a syntax extension block specifies a parser name, the corresponding
parser's function is invoked. Otherwise, all registered parsers are invoked in
order of registration until one succeeds; if a parser reports a syntax error,
the next one is attempted, but in case of a non-syntax error execution stops.
Note that if parser did not register function for the current context, it will
not be considered.

You may find definitions in src/include/parser/parser_ext.h and code in
src/backend/parser/parser_ext.c.

Design and Implementation Rationale
-----------------------------------

* Why such syntax?

  The syntax is indeed very cumbersome, but it is in the spirit of SQL
  keywords. Other options include:

  - @{ source text @}

    This would require modifying the lexer.

  - WITH <'optional parser name'> ('source text')

    This may be possible with GLR parser, but it is very brittle to implement.

* Why handle non-terminal entry points during the post-processing?

  It is true that in general these too can be processed during the grammatical
  analysis. However, it was deemed necessary to give handlers some additional
  context which they cannot infer otherwise, unlike the core parser. The
  upshot of this is ABI breakage because of the new SyntaxExtension node.

* TopMemoryContext is used for the parser registry. PortalContext was not
  sufficient here, and usage of TopMemoryContext seems like a common practise
  in cases like this.

* Instead of universal dispatch for SE_... functions a set of PARSER_TRY and
  PARSER_CATCH_SYNTAX_ERROR macros is provided. Because these functions have
  varying signatures and return types, their semantics are context-sensitive
  and differ across instances.

Future work
-----------

Since this is a prototype, only a minimal set of features was implemented.
Some functionality that is currently not supported, but could possibly be
added as incremental features:

* More entry points with rich context

  For example, src/backend/parser/parse_utilcmd.c has non-exported
  CreateStmtContext structure which would be very useful.

* Relative error position reporting in user-provided parsers

* Support for List of Nodes returned from the separate statement

* Parser priority, enable/disable parser in the current session etc.


